A||||6||mshill||Removing Front Control Arm Bushings...||||13
Z||000000||mshill||09-27-1999||03:42 PM||||This last weekend I decided to do the poly bushings on my front suspension.  The sway bar as well as both control arms came out easily.  I removed top then bottom and kept both ball joints intact.  It wasn't too bad.<P>Then the fun began.  I read that the only way to get the bushings out was to burn them and then push them out.  The tops of the driver side were OK and weren't too much trouble.  The lower control arms on the otherhand were a pain.  I ended up having to completely burn one of them out because it would not pop out.  That left a mess on the inside of the control arm.<P>On the passenger side I decided I would try another technique.  This time I tried to push the metal insert out using a gear puller and a bolt.  Worked like a charm on the uppers and just by adding a washer I was able to easily push the upper bushings out as well.  The lowers, again, were much more stubborn had to burn both of them, and yet again one had to be completely burned out.  Overall the whole thing was smell and very messy.<P>There must be a better way.||||reg||13
Z||000001||lowCG||09-27-1999||04:26 PM||||When I did the rear of my car,I drilled the bushing,between the inner and outer sleeve,then pressed out the rest with a large socket in the vise.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Richard Parnell||09-27-1999||04:34 PM||||If you have to burn out the old bushings, how on earth are you supposed to get the new bushings in?||||reg||11
Z||000003||lowCG||09-28-1999||12:40 AM||||My vise only cost me $25,new,so it's no special deal,just used a large socket,some dishwashing detergent,a few twists on the vise.<BR>Found a bargain basement performance spring the other day(by dumb luck)for the front end;a stock '86 Golf GTI  front coil,with a 1" spacer is currently rushing me around corners on my new 16X7" rims and tires,gives a 1 1/2" drop this way,with no other changes.<BR>You might want to check out some of the previous posts regarding poly bushings for install tips.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||09-28-1999||04:24 PM||||I am putting in Polyurethane bushings and they are not expandable like the rubber ones.  They are pretty much press-fit.  Grease them up and press them in with the vise.<P>I got the control arms all cleaned up and am ready to assemble the bushings and put them on the car.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Harry||09-28-1999||06:15 PM||||Are you saying that the new upper and lower control bushings dont come with an outer steel sleave (like stock replacements)?<BR>I did those in mine using the correct bushing removal tools and they went pretty smooth, however the steel control arms in the fiero models all seem to be very thin and can bend easily in the ends.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||09-28-1999||10:54 PM||||Harry's right, you must be very careful about pressing bushing (and lower ball joints) out of the control arms because the Fiero "A" frames bend easily. I recently received my new Prothane poly bushings. This brand is pretty good and cheaper than most, but you have to use the old outer metal bushing case. This means burning the old rubber out, or drilling, or pressing, or a combination of all of the above. Burning is messy and time consuming, but generally works out best in the long run. Sometimes you get lucky and the pop right out, other times they're a bitch.||||reg||1
